dzn: Close a file descriptor once its memory import succeeds

Importing memory from a file descriptor transfers the descriptor's ownership
to the Vulkan implementation, but Dozen never closed it. gfxstream hands
each guest app's import of a display buffer a duplicate of the buffer's
exported descriptor, so every import left one open, and each open
descriptor keeps its D3D12 shared allocation alive after every Vulkan object
using it is gone. Under WSL2 crosvm gained about 27 of them a minute while
3DMark ran (RTX 4080 Laptop GPU); with this change the count stays flat.

Close the descriptor after a successful import; a failed import leaves it
with the caller, as Vulkan says.

--- a/src/microsoft/vulkan/dzn_device.c
+++ b/src/microsoft/vulkan/dzn_device.c
@@ -2865,6 +2865,10 @@
 
    VkExternalMemoryHandleTypeFlags export_flags = 0;
    HANDLE import_handle = NULL;
+#ifndef _WIN32
+   /* An imported file descriptor, which a successful import owns. */
+   int import_fd = -1;
+#endif
    bool imported_from_d3d11 = false;
    void *host_pointer = NULL;
 #ifdef _WIN32
@@ -2921,6 +2925,7 @@
             return vk_error(device, VK_ERROR_INVALID_EXTERNAL_HANDLE);
          }
          import_handle = (HANDLE)(intptr_t)imp->fd;
+         import_fd = imp->fd;
          break;
       }
 #endif
@@ -3186,6 +3191,16 @@
          goto cleanup;
    }
 
+#ifndef _WIN32
+   /* Importing a file descriptor transfers its ownership to the
+    * implementation: the D3D12 object opened from it holds the memory now, and
+    * an fd left open keeps the shared allocation alive after every Vulkan
+    * object using it is gone.
+    */
+   if (import_fd >= 0)
+      close(import_fd);
+#endif
+
    *out = dzn_device_memory_to_handle(mem);
    return VK_SUCCESS;
 
